<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>





<handout>
    <title>Learning Target 13 Practice</title>

    <p >
        <em>Given a conditional statement, identify the
                            hypothesis and conclusion, determine its truth
                            value, and apply it.
        </em>
    </p>

  <paragraphs>
          <title>Need to know:</title>
  
          <p>
              <ul>
                  <li>
                      <p>
                          In a conditional statement the hypothesis is the part after
                          the if, not including the if, and the conclusion is the part
                          after the then, not including the then.
                          In <q>If <m>x</m> is even then <m>x^{2}</m> is even</q>, the
                          hypothesis is <q><m>x</m> is even</q> and the conclusion is
                          <q><m>x^{2}</m> is even</q>.
                      </p>
                  </li>
  
                  <li>
                      <p>
                          Modus ponens: If <m>P\implies Q</m> is true and <m>P</m> is
                          true then <m>Q</m> is true.
                      </p>
                  </li>
  
                  <li>
                      <p>
                          Modus tollens: If <m>P\implies Q</m> is true and <m>Q</m> is
                          false then <m>P</m> is false.
                      </p>
                  </li>
  
                  <li>
                      <p>
                          If <m>P\implies Q</m> is true, and you know <m>P</m> Is
                          false or <m>Q</m> is true, you can't conclude anything.
                      </p>
                  </li>
  
                  <li>
                      <p>
                          The truth table for <m>P\implies Q</m>.
                      </p>
                  </li>
              </ul>
          </p>
      </paragraphs>
  
      <paragraphs>
          <title>Practice:</title>
  
          <p>
              <ol>
                  <li>
                      <p>
                          Weekly Practice 10 #1
                      </p>
                  </li>
  
                  <li>
                      <p>
                          Worksheet pages 77-78
                      </p>
                  </li>
  
                  <li>
                      <p>
                          Consider the conditional statement ``If <m>n</m> is a prime
                          number then <m>n^{2}</m> has exactly three positive factors.
                          What is the hypothesis and what is the conclusion?
                          Using ONLY that the conditional statement is true, and each
                          of the following (individually) what can you conclude (if
                          anything)?
                          Explain your answer.
                          <ol>
                              <li>
                                  <p>
                                      <m>3</m> is prime,
                                  </p>
                              </li>
  
                              <li>
                                  <p>
                                      <m>6</m> is not prime,
                                  </p>
                              </li>
  
                              <li>
                                  <p>
                                      <m>5^{2}</m> has 3 positive factors
                                  </p>
                              </li>
  
                              <li>
                                  <p>
                                      <m>4^{2}</m> has 5 positive factors
                                  </p>
                              </li>
                          </ol>
                      </p>
                  </li>
  
                  <li>
                      <p>
                          Consider the following conditional statement ``If the
                          function <m>y=f(x)</m> is continuous at <m>x=a</m>, then
                          <m>\lim_{x\to a}f(x)</m> exists.'' What is the hypothesis
                          and what is the conclusion?
                          Using ONLY that the conditional statement is true, and each
                          of the following (individually) what can you conclude (if
                          anything)?
                          Explain your answer.
                          <ol>
                              <li>
                                  <p>
                                      <m>f(x)</m> is a function that is continuous at
                                      <m>x=-3</m>
                                  </p>
                              </li>
  
                              <li>
                                  <p>
                                      <m>g(x)</m> is a function that is not continuous
                                      at <m>x=7</m>
                                  </p>
                              </li>
  
                              <li>
                                  <p>
                                      for a function <m>h(x)</m>,
                                      <m>\lim_{x\to 17}h(x)</m> exists
                                  </p>
                              </li>
  
                              <li>
                                  <p>
                                      for a function <m>k(x)</m>,
                                      <m>\lim_{x\to \pi}k(x)</m> does not exist
                                  </p>
                              </li>
                          </ol>
                      </p>
                  </li>
              </ol>
          </p>
      </paragraphs>

        </handout>